370 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 181 0 R
/E (Reading a long novel carefully enough to track every character and map out how they relate to each other is genuinely hard work. It takes hours, sometimes days, and even then a reader can miss subtle patterns - characters who barely interact directly but are constantly mentioned together, or how a relationship that seems friendly in the early chapters turns hostile by the end. LitMind was built to handle this kind of analysis automatically.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
371 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 181 0 R
/E (The system takes a novel or play as plain text input and runs it through a pipeline that does three things: first, it finds all the named characters using spaCy's Named Entity Recognition and then cleans up the mess of duplicate names and aliases that any real novel produces. Second, it figures out how often pairs of characters appear near each other in the text, builds a weighted co-occurrence graph using NetworkX, and computes centrality scores to identify the characters who are most connected to the overall story. Third and this is the part that requires actual semantic understanding it calls Google's Gemini API to classify the type of relationship between the most prominent character pairs and assign a sentiment score between -1.0 \(deeply hostile\) and +1.0 \(strongly allied\).)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
372 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 181 0 R
/E (On Dracula by Bram Stoker, the system achieved an F1-score of 93.4% for character extraction when running with Gemini, compared to 72.4% for raw spaCy without any post- processing. The offline-only mode \(no API key needed\) hit 89% F1. Processing a full novel takes roughly 15-30 seconds depending on the size.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
373 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 181 0 R
/E (The frontend is a Streamlit web app with three tabs. The first shows the character network graph and a sortable table of centrality scores. The second shows Gemini's summary of the plot, genre classification, tone, and setting. The third shows book recommendations based on the user's reading history.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
374 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 181 0 R
/E (Keywords: Named Entity Recognition, Character Extraction, Co-occurrence Analysis, Graph- Based Literary Analysis, Sentiment Scoring, Large Language Model, Gemini API, Digital Humanities)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
