/P 186 0 R
/E (Here are some examples that have been tried. Nalisnick and Baird [5], for example, tried to plot the arcs of character sentiment throughout a novel by scoring sentiment on the text where each character appears, and plotting those scores as they change over time. Not a perfect solution, but it does produce interesting - maybe not so granulated, but still interesting - visualizations of how the world of a character is altered. Comparison: relationship scores in Lit Mind work the same way: They are scored from the perspective of a pair of characters that co-occur in a passage, not from all of them combined.)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
452 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 187 0 R
/E (As shown in Table 1, early computation-based methods were based on early co-occurrence assumptions that two characters would be related if they occurred in the same paragraph. That assumption breaks down when characters share the same stage physical space but are not related in the context of adversarial lives. Standard pre-trained transformer models \(BERT, RoBERTa\) are limited by their token context windows \(512 tokens\) and cannot handle books without fragmenting chunks into contextually and narratively lost chunks.)
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
453 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 187 0 R
/E (Lit Mind addresses these issues by utilizing Google's Gemini API with a long-context reasoning capability \(1 million tokens in Gemini 1.5 Pro\). This enables simultaneous context ingestion, endowing the system to fully comprehend acts and books in their entirety, while maintaining an excellent degree of accuracy in co-reference of characters. The system also provides a non-API based mode of spaCy.)
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
