678 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 197 0 R
/E (Here are some of the many reasons we chose Python as our language for building our tools and backend. Python has a more mature ecosystem of NLP and data science libraries than any other language. Our primary NLP library, SpaCy, is implemented as a Python-native library. We also built our web framework, Streamlit, using Python. The easy-to-read language of Python helped us iterate through the NLP pipeline quickly.)
/Pg 33 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
679 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 197 0 R
/E (A bottleneck for Python is large CPU-bound NLP workloads. We faced this when running spaCy over an entire novel \(which took anywhere from 10 to 30 seconds depending on model size and hardware configuration\). We mitigated this using Streamlit caching decorators \(st.cacheresource for the SpaCy model, st.cachedata for our catalogue of books\) so that we don't run this expensive step on every user interaction.)
/Pg 33 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
680 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 197 0 R
/T (7.3 SpaCy NLP Library)
/E (7.3 SpaCy NLP Library)
/Pg 33 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
681 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 197 0 R
/E (All offline NLP processing uses SpaCy 3.7+. You can select the default encorewebsm \(12 MB\) or the more extensive encorewebmd or encoreweb_lg in the sidebar if you prefer a quicker, more precise outcome. Each is scaled up in accuracy as its size increases: the larger model has better quality dependency parsing \(important for the false-positive filters\), but will also take 3-5x as long.)
/Pg 33 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
682 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 197 0 R
/E (A limitation of encoreweb_sm is that it was trained on news and web text, not nineteenth- century literature prose, which means that there is a relative loss of NER accuracy on historical language. The label-voting and dependency filters of Section 4.4 address this.)
/Pg 33 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
