712 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 198 0 R
/E (Networkx versionused for constructing and processing the character interaction graph:3.2+. An undirectedweightedgraph, where each node is a character, each two co-occurring characters are associated with one un-directededge with the normalized strength score as edge weight. The positions of all nodes on the 2D plane are generated by using spring_layout.)
/Pg 34 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
713 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 198 0 R
/E (Three metrics are given for each network: degree centrality \(the number of edges / max number of possible edges\), betweenness centrality \(the number of shortest paths through a node / total number of shortest paths\) and weighted degree \(the sum of the weights of the edges\). These are presented in a sortable table on the Streamlit page.)
/Pg 34 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
714 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 198 0 R
/T (7.7 Plotly Visualization)
/E (7.7 Plotly Visualization)
/Pg 34 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
715 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 198 0 R
/E (Uses Plotly 5.20+ for interactive network plot. The character network plot is made with a scatter chart with individual point locations set by the spring layout of a NetworkX graph, line width based on relationship strength, size of the character proportional to total mentions for the character, and character names as text labels.)
/Pg 34 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
716 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 198 0 R
/E (The Plotly figure is shown with st.plotlychart, usecontainer_width=True to fill the whole browser window. This comes with pan & zoom controls enabled by default, which is necessary with dense networks as labels can overlap at the default zoom level.)
/Pg 34 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
