<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 202 0 R
/T (These results are of interest for the novel: using the degree centrality, Quincey Morris has the highest degree \(he interacts with more different characters than anyone else\). It does not really matter that Quincey is not the "main" character of Dracula, the only thing it shows here is that Quincey Morris appears in almost every group scene in the novel. Jonathan Harker \(22\) has the highest weighted degree \(that is, some of his relationships may have had a higher weight than those of other characters, but if you take the sum of all relationships, theirs is the highest\).)
/E (These results are of interest for the novel: using the degree centrality, Quincey Morris has the highest degree \(he interacts with more different characters than anyone else\). It does not really matter that Quincey is not the "main" character of Dracula, the only thing it shows here is that Quincey Morris appears in almost every group scene in the novel. Jonathan Harker \(22\) has the highest weighted degree \(that is, some of his relationships may have had a higher weight than those of other characters, but if you take the sum of all relationships, theirs is the highest\).)
/Pg 38 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
858 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 202 0 R
/T (Count Dracula could be the very person the story is about having the lowest scores of centrality. This is because he is the subject of the story and based on the attempt of him to attack and is also in very few direct scenes with some of the other character to name \(excluding Jonathan Harker\).)
/E (Count Dracula could be the very person the story is about having the lowest scores of centrality. This is because he is the subject of the story and based on the attempt of him to attack and is also in very few direct scenes with some of the other character to name \(excluding Jonathan Harker\).)
/Pg 38 0 R
